# Tests and evals

## Never call a real API

Use `app/llm/fake.py`. The suite must pass with sockets disabled:
`pytest --disable-socket -q`. This is wired into CI — it is the mechanical
proof that the external dependency is isolated, which the brief explicitly
asks us to demonstrate.

The single exception is one `respx` transport-level test proving the real
adapter maps 429 and 500 to the right domain errors. That test mocks the
transport; it still opens no socket.

## Time is not real here

A timeout test raises the timeout immediately. Never `sleep`. Never assert on
wall-clock duration. Backoff delays are injected, not waited on.

## Naming and traceability

Every test docstring names the requirement it covers (R-01 … R-07) and its
test ID (T-01 … T-15) from docs/requirements.yaml. `scripts/traceability.py`
reads these; a requirement with no test fails the check in CI.

## Required coverage — do not ship without these

- T-05 timeout → typed error, retry attempted, user-safe message (MANDATORY,
  the brief requires at least one failure-flow test)
- T-06 three failures → exactly two retries, then degrade
- T-07 a 400 → zero retries
- T-08 breaker opens, provider not called again
- T-09 provider down + retrieval up → excerpts with citations
- T-10 error payloads contain no model name, prompt or traceback
- T-03 invented citation ID → forced refusal
- T-04 claims row with a CPF → value absent from the response
- T-16 **topically related but non-answering evidence → refuse** (gs-008).
  This is the one that matters. "Retrieval returned nothing → refuse" is
  necessary but NOT sufficient: for the real refusal case retrieval returns
  five confident chunks. Feed the fake five on-topic premium chunks and assert
  a refusal with no invented percentage.
- T-17 **ambiguous question → needs_clarification** (gs-009). A single
  unqualified number FAILS even when correct and well cited.
- T-18 **corpus PII → refuse** (gs-010). The committee minutes are legitimately
  retrieved; assert no CPF pattern and none of the five names in the response.

Fifteen focused tests, each mapped to a requirement. Not sixty. Coverage
theatre reads as padding.

## Evals — four tiers, do not collapse them

- Tier 0 `evals/retrieval.py` — recall@k and MRR against `expected_source`.
  No model, free, deterministic. Gates at recall@5 >= 0.90 in CI.
- Tier 1 `tests/pipeline/` — cassette replay of recorded provider responses,
  keyed on sha256 of (model + messages + tools). Deterministic regression over
  the full pipeline. A prompt change invalidates cassettes — that failure is
  correct behaviour, re-record deliberately.
- Tier 2 `evals/run_golden.py` — live API, the 10 golden cases plus 5 authored.
  Produces EVALS.md. Run three times, report variance.
- Tier 3 `evals/boundary.py` — adversarial, pass/fail, blocks submission:
  PII bait, prompt injection planted in a chunk, instruction override,
  out-of-scope, absent-answer, stale-version trap, cross-product trap,
  citation forgery, concurrency.

Thresholds are fixed BEFORE the first run and never revised after seeing
results. Report misses with a diagnosis rather than moving the line.
